1. Select the Pre-image option.
2. Click to generate a hash to obtain the hash value.
3. Try to guess the original input.
4. Click Submit Guess to verify the guess.
5. Observe whether the guessed input produces the required hash.
C) Second Pre-image Resistance
1. Select the Second Pre-image option.
2. Observe the original input and its hash value.
3. Enter a different input.
4. Click Check Hash.
5. Observe whether the hash values match.
D) Collision Resistance
1. Select the Collision Resistance option.
2. Choose a hashing algorithm such as SHA-256.
3. Enter a message and generate its hash.
4. Enter a different message and generate its hash.
5. Compare both hash values and observe the results.
Observation
• Hashing: A fixed hash is generated for the input message.
• Pre-image Resistance: Recovering the input from its hash is computationally difficult.
• Second Pre-image Resistance: A different input generally produces a different hash value.
• Collision Resistance: Finding two different inputs with the same hash is computationally infeasible for a
secure hash function.
Result
The experiment demonstrated the working of cryptographic hash functions and their important properties:
pre-image resistance, second pre-image resistance and collision resistance. These properties help protect data
integrity and support secure communication and authentication systems.
